    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Women's subjective experience of induced abortion was investigated in a survey of 100 women who underwent the procedure at a hospital-based family planning clinic in Quebec, Canada, in a 3-week period in December 1993. 95% of procedures involved first-trimester pregnancies. The mean age of abortion patients was 24.2 years; 82% were single and 52% were college or university students. One hour after the abortion, respondents completed a questionnaire on their experience. One-third rated their physical and emotional health as fair or poor immediately after the procedure; self-reported pre-pregnancy physical and emotional status was the strongest predictor of post-abortion status. 56% brought a support person (generally spouse/boyfriend or girlfriend) with them to the clinic; women who came alone were more likely to have had a prior abortion and reported poorer emotional health prior to the pregnancy. The mean score on a 5-point scale of preparedness for the procedure was 4.3 and adequate preparation was a strong predictor of a high concordance between expectations and the actual experience of abortion. Adequate preparation was also negatively related to perceived pain intensity. Nurses, and to a lesser extent physicians, were rated as very supportive during the procedure. 56% of abortion patients indicated they were very satisfied with their experience, 31% were moderately satisfied, and 12% were unsure. The strongest predictor of satisfaction was the subject's rating on the adequacy of preparation prior to abortion with regard to the nature and duration of the procedure, pain intensity, and social support. Satisfaction was higher for women accompanied by a support person. These findings confirm the importance of pre-abortion education and psychosocial counseling to enhance preparedness for the procedure."
    }
